  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 241 PM CDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nLines of cumulus clouds have developed near and south of the \nMissouri River Valley this afternoon. Without much upper level \nsupport to get storms going, we will need to rely on spots \nreaching convective temperatures to get storms to initiate. \nAccording to soundings, this will be around 95 degrees or so. \nChances for storm development with this is low, but either way \nsevere weather is unlikely given weak mid level lapse rates and \nweak shear. However, can't rule out a wind gust up to 55 mph in \nany collapsing thunderstorm if they even develop in our area in\nthe first place. Otherwise another very warm and humid \nafternoon continues, with heat indices pushing 100 degrees at \ntimes especially west of I-29 into the Sioux City metro. \nDoesn't look like these 100 degree heat indices will be too \nwidespread and dew points do look to drop off a bit more through\nthe afternoon, so did continue to hold off on any heat \nheadlines. However, it's still important to be hydrated and take\nsome breaks when working or playing outdoors this afternoon! \n\nShowers and storms over western South Dakota will be knocking on \nsouth-central South Dakota's door by daybreak Thursday; \nhowever, this activity will likely be weakening as it moves into\nthe area. Additional showers and storms will likely develop as \nan upper level wave moves across the area and will likely have \nenough with it to break the cap in the fairly weakly capped\nenvironment. This morning activity is unlikely to be severe and\nshould help limit full destabilization from occurring before \nany afternoon/evening activity develops. However, we will still \nlikely see a line of stronger thunderstorms develop in the \nafternoon over central South Dakota and move southeastward \nacross the area through the evening hours associated with a cool\nfront. 65 mph winds and quarter sized hail will be the main \nthreat with this afternoon/evening activity. \n\nThere is an alternate, but less likely scenario where we don't \nget much if any rainfall during the Thursday morning time frame \ndue to a stronger than expected cap, allowing for more \ndestabilization to occur. In this scenario, like shown in the \n29.12z HRRR, a stronger line/MCS would develop in the afternoon \nand evening and bring wind gusts potentially up to around 65 \nmph. Trends will be monitored, so keep up to date with the \nlatest forecast. A secondary wave and an upper level low will \nclose off near the area Thursday night into early Friday \nmorning, likely keeping general showers and thunderstorms \ncontinuing into the night. This will also lead to some flash \nflooding concerns Thursday afternoon into the night. Wet grounds\nfrom morning showers/storms will combine with the stronger \nafternoon storms and overnight storms to lead to some flash \nflooding concerns mainly in urban areas that see multiple \nthunderstorms. Rainfall totals through Thursday night/early \nFriday morning will be around a half an inch to 1.5\", but \nlocalized amounts exceeding 4+ inches will be possible. Make \nsure to stay weather aware and keep up to date with the latest \nforecast! \n\nAfter this next storm system passes, an upper level ridge will build \ninto the area and help to keep us dry. And with northerly flow in \nplace, high temperatures for Friday and Saturday will be a lot more \npleasant with some areas especially on Friday having highs staying \nonly in the 70s! Southerly flow returns again by Sunday, so we \nshould start to see another warm up, though we should stay below \nheat advisory criteria even as highs warm back up to the middle 80s \nto lower 90s. The next appreciable rain chances will return Monday \nnight into Tuesday as cold front moves into the area. It looks like \nthere may be enough instability and shear for at least some severe \nthreat, but uncertainty remains high at this time.",
